================================================================================
2. License
================================================================================

The LICENSE file at the root of this repository is the license as received
from upstream. It has never been modified by Hanzo — its last change is an
upstream commit by Marc Seitz (bf2cb41ca, 2025-12-08).

That file sets out a split arrangement, which Hanzo has preserved:

  * Content under the `ee/` and `app/(ee)/` directories is under a separate
    commercial license, not the AGPL. See section 3 below.
  * Third-party components are under the original license provided by the
    owner of the applicable component. See section 5 below.
  * Everything else is under the GNU Affero General Public License,
    Version 3 (AGPL-3.0), whose full text is included in that same file.

AGPL-3.0 section 13 — remote network interaction
------------------------------------------------

Hanzo Dataroom is made available to users over a network at
https://dataroom.hanzo.ai. Section 13 of the AGPL therefore applies to the
AGPL-licensed portion: users interacting with the software remotely must be
given an opportunity to receive the Corresponding Source of the running
version.

The Corresponding Source is published at:

    https://github.com/hanzoai/dataroom


================================================================================
3. Commercially licensed directories (`ee/`, `app/(ee)/`)
================================================================================

The `ee/` directory, and `app/(ee)/` which symlinks its license, are NOT
under the AGPL. They carry a separate commercial license in `ee/LICENSE.md`.
This split originates upstream: Papermark shipped these directories under
"The Papermark Commercial License".

Record of what Hanzo changed here, stated plainly:

  In commit eb2273543 (2026-03-01, "Rebrand from Papermark to Hanzo
  Dataroom"), `ee/LICENSE.md` was edited as part of a repository-wide
  rebranding pass. The edit retitled the document from "The Papermark
  Commercial License" to "The Hanzo Dataroom Commercial License" and changed
  its copyright line from `Copyright (c) 2023-present Papermark, Inc.` to
  `Copyright (c) 2023-present Hanzo AI, Inc.`, together with the
  corresponding party names in the body. The operative terms were otherwise
  unchanged.

  The code in `ee/` is not merely "substantially" upstream Papermark code —
  it is entirely upstream. Every one of the 160 file-additions ever made
  under `ee/` was authored upstream: 151 by Marc Seitz <marc@mfts.io> and 9
  by other upstream contributors. Zero files under `ee/` have ever been
  authored by Hanzo, and no Hanzo commit has added source there. This is
  verifiable with:

      git log --format='%H %ae' --name-status --diff-filter=A -- ee

  Retitling the license document and substituting the copyright holder does
  not transfer copyright in that code, which remains with Papermark, Inc.
  and its contributors.

  This is flagged as an open question requiring review by legal counsel. It
  is recorded here rather than silently corrected, because unwinding it is a
  legal determination and not an engineering one. Note also that the root
  LICENSE directs readers to "ee/LICENSE" while the file is in fact named
  `ee/LICENSE.md`, and that it identifies the covered directories by their
  upstream URLs under github.com/mfts/papermark.

  Deletion of `ee/` was evaluated on 2026-08-03 as a way of resolving this
  by removing the code rather than adjudicating its ownership. It was not
  carried out, because `ee/` is not a severable commercial add-on in this
  codebase: 141 files outside `ee/` import from it, and those imports include
  core infrastructure rather than paywall gating alone. In particular
  `ee/features/storage/config.ts` supplies the S3 configuration consumed by
  `lib/files/aws-client.ts`, `lib/files/bulk-download-presign.ts` and every
  document view and download route. Deleting `ee/` therefore removes document
  storage, AI document chat, conversations, granular permissions, dataroom
  invitations, workflows and templates, and leaves a tree that does not
  build. The licensing question above is unchanged by this and remains open.


================================================================================
4. Modifications by Hanzo
================================================================================

    Copyright 2026 Hanzo AI, Inc.

Hanzo's modifications begin 2026-03-01. Modifications to AGPL-licensed
portions are licensed under AGPL-3.0, on the same terms as the upstream work.
In summary, Hanzo has:

  * Rebranded from Papermark to Hanzo Dataroom — metadata, titles,
    descriptions, Open Graph tags, email templates, the powered-by component,
    the x-powered-by header, legal references and social handles — and
    repointed papermark.io/.com URLs to dataroom.hanzo.ai.
  * Replaced authentication with Hanzo IAM: an OIDC SSO provider, IAM-only
    sign-in, and an `org_id` claim carried into the JWT and session.
  * Replaced Upstash with Hanzo KV (an ioredis wire-compatible client).
  * Replaced PostHog product analytics with Hanzo Insights.
  * Upgraded to Next.js 15 and TypeScript 5.9, with the associated async
    `params` / `searchParams` and Edge Runtime corrections.
  * Removed vendored third-party files (a tracked vendor directory and
    vendored Handsontable files) in favour of declared dependencies.
  * Added a Dockerfile and moved CI/CD to the canonical Hanzo reusable
    workflow, with credentials sourced from Hanzo KMS.


